A calorie deficit makes the same warm-up feel heavier. If you only remember how the session felt, you will think you are weaker every week. If you log the work, you can see whether you are actually losing training quality or just losing glycogen and patience.

PushLab is a training log. It does not read Apple Health and it is not a medical device.

## Log the same things you logged in a surplus

- Date and session type
- Exercise names you will still use in four weeks
- Sets, reps, and load
- A one-line note when something unusual happened (bad sleep, heat, rest-day scale spike)

Do not invent new exercise names every session. “Lat pulldown” and “pulldown machine” are the same movement if you want a trend.

## Review on a weekly cadence, not after every pump

Once a week, look at:

1. Planned sessions vs completed sessions
1. Volume on two or three main lifts
1. Any personal bests (they get rarer on a cut; holding load is a win)
1. Extra cardio that appeared because the scale stalled

If completed sessions drop, the cut is too aggressive or life got busy. Fix that before you add more work.

## What “progress” looks like while losing fat

- You still show up
- Loads on compound lifts hold within a small band
- Reps at a given load do not collapse
- Conditioning stays repeatable (same loop, similar time)

PRs can still happen early in a cut if you were under-recovered before. Later, maintenance of strength is the goal. The scale can fall while the log stays flat. That pairing is success.

## Do not let the bathroom or the scale write the program

More urine in week one, a rest-day jump, a glycogen refill after a wedding—none of those belong in the load prescription. Put them in a note if they messed with your head. Then run the session you planned.

Random extra sets “to earn food” pollute the log. You will not know whether you recovered. Write the extra work as its own session if you insist on doing it, so the main template stays comparable.

## A template you can reuse

Keep one full-body or split template for the whole cut. Change load and reps, not the menu, unless something hurts. Copy last week. Adjust one lever: a little more load, a little less volume, or the same.

That loop is the product: log in the gym, including offline if the floor has no signal, then check volume and consistency later. Feelings are allowed. They do not get to erase the numbers.
